# File names under @@ROOTS_HUMAN@@

Every tracked file under @@ROOTS_HUMAN@@ carries a @@RULE@@ basename, matching
`@@REGEX@@`.

Exempt from the rule:

- the basenames @@EXEMPT_BASENAMES_HUMAN@@, conventional spellings tooling and
  forges look for exactly
- the paths @@EXEMPT_PATHS_HUMAN@@
- the extensions @@EXEMPT_EXTENSIONS_HUMAN@@, whose casing belongs to the
  language rather than to this rule

No two tracked paths under the roots may differ only by case. A
case-insensitive checkout writes the second over the first, so such a pair is a
corrupted tree rather than a style question.

`@@SCRIPT_PATH@@` is the gate. It runs over the tracked set and is what actually
enforces this; a path-scoped rule loads when a covered file is READ, never when
one is created, so this file cannot catch a new name on its own.

Renaming an existing file is `/docs-hygiene:audit-file-names` to plan it and
`/docs-hygiene:realign-file-names` to apply it, one acceptance per file: the
rename is the small half, and repointing every citation of it is the rest.
